Z||000019||Cutlass207TDC||11-04-2001||05:50 PM||||if you think that an air-intake or ram-air is fake.  You dont know anything about cars.  When nascar puts restrictor plates on, they cut down on how much air is going in the vehicles.  If you plan on having a fast car,(modified)plan on having an <BR>air-intake or ram-air.  It will do you so much better than the stock air box.||||reg||1
Z||000020||Jay||11-04-2001||06:33 PM||||<P>JAY: Do you have any pics? Do you use a carb or TPI? I plan to use a carb on my 3.1 build and have not figured out how to get the engine cool air.<P>Pics can be seen on my site: <A HREF="http://www.members.home.net/jaygrande" TARGET=_blank>www.members.home.net/jaygrande</A> <BR>I'm using a stock 1997 Cadillac Northstar engine.  The only mod is RAM AIR which some don't believe works.  I got the biggest K&N and put it where it will get the coolest air.  It does work, I've got the track slips to prove it.<P><P>------------------<BR>Jay<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/newsig2_copy_1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000021||Smokin' GT||11-04-2001||07:11 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Cutlass207TDC:<BR><B>if you think that an air-intake or ram-air is fake.  You dont know anything about cars.  When nascar puts restrictor plates on, they cut down on how much air is going in the vehicles.  If you plan on having a fast car,(modified)plan on having an <BR>air-intake or ram-air.  It will do you so much better than the stock air box.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I didn't say anything about air-intake, I'm strictly talking about the concept or "RAM-AIR" the concept according to physics is not plausible (this is pertaining mainly to preformance).  The idea of course is to compress air, and in order to compress air it must traveling at VERY high speeds, as that of a turbo, which spins at 1000's of RPM.  But, a car traveling at its top speed could not compress even close to what it would require to gain performance.  <P>And in reality, the shape of the scoops do not in fact compress air.  As most RAM air scoops they're shaped with a larger opening and end up get smaller towards the end.  This is not condusive to faster moving air.  The only design of a scoop which could actually speed up the air (once again not at speeds a car could produce) would be to have a small opening and gradually get wider.<P>I read all of this a while back in an article by a physics major. Your flame was noted.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Smokin' GT (edited 11-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000022||p4n1c||11-04-2001||07:15 PM||||And of course we must believe everything we read because they have a title beside their name.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_bs.gif"> <P>It's like saying that because someone has their MCSE or CCNA, they know everything about networking.<p>[This message has been edited by p4n1c (edited 11-04-2001).]||||reg||4
Z||000023||Doni Hagan||11-04-2001||08:30 PM||||Are you guys kidding? Holley scoops are going for $100 on EBAY? Hell, I've got my old one ($17.00 from Fiero Store years ago)laying around in my garage somewhere. NO WAY I'd pay $100 for another one. I painted mine the same color as the car (silver), took the Holley" name off and replaced it with a "2.8" emblem from a Chevy. I ordered Aus' scoops so I figured I wouldn't need it any more anyway. ||||reg||1
Z||000024||Cutlass207TDC||11-04-2001||08:44 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Smokin' GT:<BR><B>  I didn't say anything about air-intake, I'm strictly talking about the concept or "RAM-AIR" the concept according to physics is not plausible (this is pertaining mainly to preformance).  The idea of course is to compress air, and in order to compress air it must traveling at VERY high speeds, as that of a turbo, which spins at 1000's of RPM.  But, a car traveling at its top speed could not compress even close to what it would require to gain performance.  <P>And in reality, the shape of the scoops do not in fact compress air.  As most RAM air scoops they're shaped with a larger opening and end up get smaller towards the end.  This is not condusive to faster moving air.  The only design of a scoop which could actually speed up the air (once again not at speeds a car could produce) would be to have a small opening and gradually get wider.<P>I read all of this a while back in an article by a physics major. Your flame was noted.<P><BR>[This message has been edited by Smokin' GT (edited 11-04-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Ram-air is not intended to be a turbo.  All it is an opening to the somewhat stock air-intake to get a little more horsepower.  This helps because it lets the cold air get directly to the filter.  There is nothin in its way. <BR>||||reg||13
Z||000025||Tigger||11-04-2001||10:18 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Smokin' GT:<BR><B>As most RAM air scoops they're shaped with a larger opening and end up get smaller towards the end.  This is not condusive to faster moving air.  The only design of a scoop which could actually speed up the air (once again not at speeds a car could produce) would be to have a small opening and gradually get wider.<P>I read all of this a while back in an article by a physics major. Your flame was noted.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Wrong!<P>Flow velocities increase when converging and decrease in diverging flows.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/crower.jpg"> <BR>That explains this - velocity stacks.<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/john_force.jpg">   <BR>John Force/Austin Coil/Bernie Fedderly<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/monaco18.jpg">   <BR>Michael Schumacher/Every F1-IRL-Cart teams<P>Tell me these guys have it all wrong too.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 11-04-2001).]||||reg||3
